Yes, Xray-core natively supports Unix Domain Sockets (UDS). You can use UDS for both inbound configurations (listening on a socket file) and fallback destinations (passing traffic locally to Nginx or HAProxy).🛠️ Inbound Configuration (Listening on a UDS)To make Xray listen natively on a Unix Domain socket file instead of a network port, provide an absolute file path in the "listen" field. You can also append file permissions (like ,0666) directly to the path to resolve user privilege issues.When using a socket path, Xray ignores the "port" field. However, some graphical user interfaces require a placeholder number (like 0 or 1) to pass validation.json{
  "inbounds": [
    {
      "listen": "/dev/shm/xray_vless.sock,0666",
      "port": 0, 
      "protocol": "vless",
      "settings": {
        "clients": [
          {
            "id": "YOUR_UUID_HERE",
            "flow": "xtls-rprx-vision"
          }
        ],
        "decryption": "none"
      },
      "streamSettings": {
        "network": "tcp",
        "tcpSettings": {
          "acceptProxyProtocol": true
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
Use code with caution.🔀 Fallback / Destination Configuration (Passing to UDS)If you use Xray as your front-end reverse proxy (e.g., using REALITY), you can natively forward decrypted fallback traffic over a Unix Domain Socket directly to a local web server. This avoids the latency of the local loopback network stack.json"streamSettings": {
  "network": "tcp",
  "security": "reality",
  "realitySettings": {
    "dest": "/run/nginx/nginx.sock",
    "xver": 1,
    "serverNames": ["example.com"],
    "privateKey": "YOUR_PRIVATE_KEY"
  }
}
Use code with caution.⚠️ Critical Implementation DetailsAbstract Domain Sockets: Standard file-based sockets inside Linux do not automatically un-link (delete) when Xray terminates, which can throw bind errors on restart. To prevent this entirely, use an abstract socket by adding an @ symbol at the beginning of your path (e.g., "listen": "@xray_abstract.sock"). Abstract sockets exist only in memory and clean up automatically.Performance optimization: Use /dev/shm/ (shared memory RAM-disk) for file-based sockets to completely bypass hard drive or SSD read/write bottlenecks.Protocol constraints: UDS configurations apply only to stream/TCP-based transports (such as standard TCP, WebSocket, or gRPC). UDP-based transports like mKCP are not supported through Unix sockets.

